# Production host identity — Biznet is production, Hostinger is frozen (rule 220)

**Sprint record:** `docs/sprints/dms-post-migration-dev-infra-alignment-1.md`.
**Authority:** `deploy/production-host.conf`. **Guide:** `docs/runbooks/production-host-identity.md`.
**Enforced by:** `tests/Feature/Cicd/ProductionHostIdentityGuardTest.php`, `scripts/production-target-check.sh`,
`App\Support\Cicd\SelfHostedRunnerScanner::deployIsolationPosture()`.

## 1. Which host is production

1. Production is the Biznet Gio host `DaengtisiaMS` at `139.190.106.38`, app at `/var/www/asia-dental-lab-v2`,
   canonical URL `https://daengtisia.online`. DNS for the domain resolves to that address since 2026-11-03.
2. The Hostinger host `srv1730088` at `145.79.13.224` is WRITE-FROZEN: a historical migration source only. Never
   deploy, migrate, restart, write, back up from or restore to it, and never reactivate it.
3. "Biznet" alone does not mean production. The heavy CI runner `daengtisia-ci-biznet` is also a Biznet machine and
   is never a deploy target. Production is only the host named in item 1 above.
4. `deploy/production-host.conf` is the one authority. A host change edits that file first, in a reviewed change;
   the guard suite then fails until CLAUDE.md CG-14, this rule and the guide agree.

## 2. Reaching it

5. The only production alias is `daengtisiams-biznet-prod`: `dmsadmin@139.190.106.38`, `StrictHostKeyChecking yes`,
   a dedicated known-hosts file holding exactly the pinned ED25519 key. Root has no SSH login; commands that need
   root run through `sudo -n`. The application runtime identity stays `daengtisiams`
   (`deploy/runtime-identity.conf`).
6. The alias `daengtisiams-vps` points at the frozen Hostinger host as root. Its name misleads, so it is never a
   production alias. Older documents that tell you to connect through that alias record the Hostinger era;
   translate them through the guide and never replay them.
7. Before any sensitive operation (write, restart, migrate, deploy, rollback, secret or clinical read) run
   `bash scripts/production-target-check.sh --remote` and proceed only on `PRODUCTION_TARGET=VERIFIED`. The check
   never accepts or refreshes a host key, and it opens no connection without `--remote`.

## 3. Host-key trust

8. Never set `StrictHostKeyChecking` to `no` or `accept-new` for production, never use `/dev/null` as its known-hosts
   file, and never enable `UpdateHostKeys` or `VerifyHostKeyDNS` for it.
9. Never seed or refresh a production host key with `ssh-keyscan`, because trust-on-first-use accepts whatever
   answers.
10. A host-key mismatch is a stop-and-escalate event. Confirm the new fingerprint out of band with the owner, then
   update `deploy/production-host.conf` and the pinned file in the same reviewed change.

## 4. CI and the deploy chain

11. No GitHub workflow opens a remote session to any server, reads a `VPS_*` secret or names a deploy or rollback
   program (`config/ci_runner.php` `forbidden_workflow_remote_access_markers`, judged on executable lines). The
   retired `deploy-vps.yml` fails closed. It stays only because the CICD-CTRL-3 layer inspects it.
12. Deploys run on the production host through the detached runner (CG-15). On Biznet that means
   `sudo -n bash scripts/deploy-vps-runner.sh start`. Every deploy needs a written owner authorization that names the
   commit. The first Biznet deploy ran on 2026-11-04 (deploy `20261104-151042`, commit `6f22beb7`) after the guide's
   §6 readiness checklist was closed; every later deploy follows the guide's §6b.
13. The deploy and rollback programs restart only `php8.3-fpm` and reload only `nginx`. They never enable, start or
   restart the queue worker, a backup timer or any other `daengtisiams-*` unit. Those are owner-controlled; the
   worker is intentionally off on Biznet.
13a. Before their first mutation, the deploy and the rollback run `dms_production_host_preflight` from the snapshot:
   positive host identity from `deploy/production-host.conf`, refusal of any frozen hostname or address, root, and
   the full toolchain. A refusal means nothing was backed up, checked out or migrated. Never add an environment
   variable that lets a run skip or shorten it.
13b. A runner status is proof only when SIX facts agree (`scripts/lib/deploy-runner-evidence.sh`): production host
   identity, UTC start/finish, deployment identifier, target commit (one pin, the deploy's HEAD == target, checkout
   still at it), runner version (`runner_contract=2` + runner SHA-256) and result (status == record, preflight GO and
   `DEPLOY OK` in the run's own log). Everything else exits `4` (`UNBOUND`, `FOREIGN`, `FROZEN`, `NOT-PRODUCTION`,
   `INCOMPATIBLE`, `INCOMPLETE`, `MISMATCHED`, `MALFORMED`, `UNVERIFIED`, `STALE`) and is never GO. Verify a run by
   its identifier (`deploy-vps-runner.sh verify <id>`); never read `latest.status` raw.
13d. The first Biznet deploy followed owner-selected Option A (`docs/runbooks/production-host-identity.md` §6a):
   quarantine the copied pre-binding evidence (move, never delete; manifest-verified), deploy, then prove that one
   unbound run with `verify-transition <id> <MERGE_SHA>`. It ran once, on 2026-11-04 (508 entries quarantined,
   deploy `20261104-151042` → `TRANSITION DEPLOY VERIFIED`). It is never run again: every later run is bound and
   proven with `verify <id>`. Never copy an old status back, never edit evidence, never reverse the quarantine by hand.
13f. The runner pins the tip of the base branch at launch, not a commit passed to it. Before `start`, confirm from
   the host that the tip is the owner-authorized commit, and push nothing to the base in between.
13e. The deploy and the rollback refuse a toolchain whose versions differ from CI (Node 22, npm ≥ 10, PHP CLI 8.3,
   Composer 2) before any mutation; Ubuntu's archive Node 18 is refused.
13c. The provisioner applies an identity change to the queue worker with `systemctl try-restart`, never
   `restart`, `start` or `enable`.

## 5. Records

14. Historical sprint records, evidence files and the CLAUDE.md archive keep their Hostinger names. They describe what
   was true when written and are never bulk-rewritten to the new host.
15. Facts in the authority file are public: the address is in DNS and the host-key fingerprint is offered to every
   client. Keep host security posture details, such as sudo policy or daemon configuration, out of this public
   repository, in the owner's private records.
